Most functions *read* data. A few *run* it. `eval("2 * 21")` returns 42 because Python parses the string as code and executes it. That is the point of `eval` (evaluate), and it is why the injection course's rule applies with full force: when data reaches an interpreter, data becomes code. Python has three common ways to hand an interpreter to a stranger:

- `eval()` and `exec()`, which run a string as Python;
- `pickle.loads()`, which rebuilds objects from bytes and may call functions while doing so;
- `yaml.load()` with an unsafe loader, which can build arbitrary Python objects.

The weakness names are CWE-94 (code injection) and CWE-502 (deserialization of untrusted data). *Serialization* turns an object into bytes for storage or transport; *deserialization* turns the bytes back into an object.

